edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0405b19e-98fb-5a56-97e4-d2d44340c6ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186103831 #edb/ref 1080 "x - 2x^{1/2} + C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186103831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186103831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14506e14-75d8-5464-ba2e-4e8d416dcb89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186103832 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\ln \\mid x \\mid - 2x^{1/2} + C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186103832 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186103832 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3737381e-42d1-5ec5-a0be-6e2c2c114442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186103833 #edb/ref 1080 "\\ln \\mid x \\mid + 2x^{1/2} + C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186103833 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186103833 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "72930a31-c1dd-529c-95f0-0c9ed79b8c26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105573 #edb/ref 1069 "e-374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105573 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\left(\\dfrac 2 x\\right)\\, \\textrm{d} x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "625c532a-d455-5b15-880d-e9fc5ae8ee72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105573 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105574 #edb/ref 1069 "e-375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105574 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the integral of $\\dfrac 1 {2x} + 3x$ with respect to $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105574 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c57c69e-efa2-5e82-992c-53570e61af09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105574 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡|x|+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{2}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105575 #edb/ref 1069 "e-376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105575 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105575 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf47e35e-da86-5f88-b1e7-0cc731c37b31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105575 #edb/ref 1074 "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105715 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105715 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338893