2186485654 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485676 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485654 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485654 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{26}{3x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485654 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "045c9a04-0733-55bf-8771-d8bc70944d9f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485654 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{26}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{26}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{26}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{26}{3}\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{26}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|+\\frac{26}{3}\\ln K \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485661 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485681 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485661 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485661 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{19}{2x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485661 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3804d021-7f16-5da7-b098-000b906fd834" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485661 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{19}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{19}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{19}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\ln|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{19}{2}\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{19}{2}\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{19}{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{19}{2}\\ln|x|+\\frac{19}{2}\\ln K \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485678 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485655 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485678 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485678 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{5}{2x})}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485678 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "945acb46-c0e6-527b-9ef1-1687fa58f759" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485678 #edb/ref 1074 "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{5}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{25}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{25}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+5\\ln|x|+\\frac{25{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+5\\ln|x|-\\frac{25}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=5\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $5\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{5}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln|x|+5\\ln K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{25}{4x}+5\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485683 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485700 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485683 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186485683 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{31}{2x})}^{