"1227ed44-e539-58ab-a920-6350e880868c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573842 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573842 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573842 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186573642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573842 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c4bd31d5-cd9b-5809-9466-5a14c329db97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573864 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573864 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573864 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186573436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573864 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc9d886d-2453-589c-b70a-85755f09affc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573896 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573896 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573896 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573896 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573896 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73a6be96-270e-560a-af2f-74b977e32a10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573896 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573909 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573909 #edb/ref 1069 "q-29840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573909 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573909 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the antiderivative of $(\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573909 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8ac5ab9-a2e7-5a8d-9104-00d51ece29df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573909 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the sum and constant factor rules and take the coefficients out of the integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=10\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})\\text{d}x & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =10(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573922 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573922 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573922 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573922 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the antiderivative of ${(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c14c2cc2-7d97-5dae-839b-1375800e6971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186573922 #edb/ref 1074 "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{4}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
